    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "it's weird though, the congestion doesn't seem to follow any traffic patterns. it just happens randomly, even at 3am when the subnet is basically idle. i checked the interface stats on 192.168.45.1 and the error counters aren't climbing, just the jitter. could it be something more subtle, like a physical layer issue or something interfering with the signal?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Since the jitter occurs randomly even when idle and without rising Rx/Drop counters, it is unlikely to be a typical hardware failure or simple congestion. The only reason physical layer interference would cause this without signal strength degradation is if the device is broken and failing packets during sleep states or wake-up cycles. You should check for a faulty NIC, a failing port, or even a rogue switch port using incorrect duplex settings on the cabling between the devices.",
      "source": "target",
